The phrase "David and Goliath" has taken on a more popular meaning denoting an underdog situation, a contest wherein a smaller, weaker opponent faces a much bigger, stronger adversary. ||| David and Goliath confront each other, Goliath with his armor and javelin, David with his staff and sling. ||| David defeats Goliath, the Philistines flee the battlefield. ||| David, then a young shepherd, takes up the challenge and kills Goliath with a stone slung from a sling. ||| == Structure of the David and Goliath narrative ==
The Goliath story is made up of base-narrative with numerous additions made probably after the exile:

 Original story
The Israelites and Philistines face each other; Goliath makes his challenge to single combat;
David volunteers to fight Goliath;
David selects five smooth stones from a creek-bed to be used in his sling;
David's courage strengthens others and eventually others defeat four other giants, possibly brothers, but relatives, reference 2 Samuel 21:15–22.

